{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: Yes","reason":"One pair of opposite sides being both parallel and equal is sufficient to establish that a quadrilateral is a parallelogram.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_184_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"m1","label":"Apply a parallelogram criterion","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_184"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2:3:2:3","reason":"Ratio D gives angles 72°, 108°, 72°, and 108°. Both pairs of opposite angles are equal, establishing a parallelogram.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_184_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p185"],"id":"cnm_184_q4_m1","label":"Equal opposite angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_184_q4_m1":["cnm_184"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_p185"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"22","reason":"AB and CD are parallel and equal, so ABCD is a parallelogram. Hence AD=BC=4, and its perimeter is 7+4+7+4=22.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_184_q5","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cnm_184_q5_m1","label":"Parallelogram recognition and perimeter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_184_q5_m1":["cnm_184","cnm_183"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_183"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. (1) or (2) only","reason":"The vectors OP and QR are equal, as are OQ and PR. Noncollinear P and Q therefore give a parallelogram; collinear position vectors give a straight-line figure.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Uses the exclusive definition of trapezoid: exactly one pair of parallel sides. An inclusive definition would change the choice.","The answer is D when a trapezoid has exactly one pair of parallel sides. With the inclusive definition, parallelograms are also trapezoids and E would apply."],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"diagonal-midpoints","label":"Compare diagonal midpoints","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diagonal-midpoints":["cnm_055","cnm_184"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_184"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_067","cnm_053"],"id":"coordinate-translations","label":"Compare opposite sides using translations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-translations":["cnm_086","cnm_072","cnm_184","cnm_p105"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_086","cnm_072","cnm_184","cnm_p105","cnm_082","cnm_067","cnm_053"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, PQRS is a parallelogram","reason":"PQ is perpendicular to BE and RS to DE; because B,E,D are collinear, PQ is parallel to RS. Likewise QR is perpendicular to CE and PS to AE, so QR is parallel to PS. Both pairs of opposite sides are parallel.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_265","cnm_055","cnm_064"],"id":"use-four-perpendicular-bisectors","label":"Use the shared-segment perpendicular bisectors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-four-perpendicular-bisectors":["cnm_184","cnm_133","cnm_068"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_133","cnm_068","cnm_265","cnm_055","cnm_064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C","reason":"AB parallel to CD together with AD=BC allows an isosceles trapezoid with unequal bases. Conditions A, B, and D are sufficient parallelogram criteria.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_184_q2","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p105"],"id":"m1","label":"Use a trapezoid counterexample","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_184","cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_071","cnm_p105"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"5","reason":"Set 2x+1=3x-4 to obtain x=5. Then AB=CD=11, so this opposite-side pair is parallel and equal.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_184_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"id":"cnm_184_q3_m1","label":"Parallel and equal opposite sides","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_184_q3_m1":["cnm_184","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_042","cnm_183"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 80°","reason":"Let H be the midpoint of PG. Then MH is parallel to AG, HN is parallel to PU, and MH = HN = 1/2. Angle MHN is 36° + 56° = 92°, so the base angles are 44°. The requested acute angle is 180° - 56° - 44° = 80°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"parallelogram-midsegment","label":"Complete a parallelogram","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallelogram-midsegment":["cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_185","cnm_137","cnm_069","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_185","cnm_137","cnm_069","cnm_115"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"7","reason":"OA=OC, OB=OD and ∠AOB=∠COD. SAS gives △AOB≅△COD, hence CD=AB=7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_123_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"bisecting-diagonals","label":"Parallelogram diagonals","nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_183"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_180"],"id":"solution:bisecting-diagonals:03b82332ea8d","label":"Recognize a parallelogram","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bisecting-diagonals":["cnm_055","cnm_184","cnm_183"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_180"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D only for the nondegenerate configuration; no listed answer describes the degenerate valid rectangle.","reason":"For width w and height h, the four relevant intersections are (w/2,w/(2sqrt(3))), (w/2,h-w/(2sqrt(3))), (h/(2sqrt(3)),h/2), and (w-h/(2sqrt(3)),h/2). Their sides are equal in a nondegenerate configuration. At w=sqrt(3), h=1, all four lie on y=1/2 and two coincide. A valid rectangle therefore need not produce a quadrilateral, contradicting the word always.","status":"confirmed_conflict"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["If w/h=√3 or 1/√3, two intersection points coincide. Thus 'always' needs a nondegeneracy qualification for the keyed quadrilateral answer."],"questionId":"1953_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"rectangle-reflection-symmetry","label":"Use the rectangle's reflection axes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rectangle-reflection-symmetry":["cnm_132","cnm_184"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_132","cnm_184"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"trisector-coordinates","label":"Locate the intersections with coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"trisector-coordinates":["cnm_084","cnm_277","cnm_184"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_277","cnm_184"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"70°","reason":"Triangles ABC and CDA have AB=CD, BC=DA and common side AC. By SSS they are congruent, so ∠D=∠B=70°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_122_q5","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"parallelogram-opposite-angles","label":"Use parallelogram properties","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallelogram-opposite-angles":["cnm_184","cnm_183"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_183"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, HE = HG is not necessarily correct","reason":"Midpoint relations give AEHF a parallelogram, BH = CD, G the midpoint of BH, and FG = 3AB/4. For A = (0,0), B = (4,0), C = (0,4), the construction gives HE = 2 but HG = √5, disproving B in general.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1955_AMC_12_Q48","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"id":"midpoint-geometry","label":"Use midpoint geometry","nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_269","cnm_055"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_067","cnm_110","cnm_180"],"id":"solution:midpoint-geometry:bcecd501ec15","label":"Use midpoint geometry","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-geometry":["cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_185","cnm_174","cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_185","cnm_174","cnm_071","cnm_067","cnm_110","cnm_180"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_174","cnm_110"],"id":"coordinate-check","label":"Represent the construction with coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-check":["cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_084","cnm_h087","cnm_h173","cnm_184","cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_084","cnm_h087","cnm_h173","cnm_184","cnm_071","cnm_082","cnm_174","cnm_110"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, a rectangle that is not a square","reason":"The four points are (3,4), (4,3), (-3,-4), and (-4,-3). Opposite points form diagonals of length 10 with common midpoint (0,0), so the quadrilateral is a rectangle. Its adjacent side lengths are √2 and 7√2, so it is not a square.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1956_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"id":"equal-bisecting-diameters","label":"Use equal bisecting diameters","nodeIds":["cnm_249","cnm_h176","cnm_184"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"],"id":"solution:equal-bisecting-diameters:4ce33274d5a3","label":"Method 1: Use the circle's diameters","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-bisecting-diameters":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"],"id":"solve-and-check-slopes","label":"Method 2: Check the side slopes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-and-check-slopes":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h167","cnm_h168","cnm_184","cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h167","cnm_h168","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: O' remains stationary","reason":"Set A=(0,0), B=(L,0), and G=(t,0), taking the construction above AB. Then A'=(0,t) and B'=(L,L-t), whose midpoint is always (L/2,L/2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q50","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_064"],"id":"fixed-parallelogram-diagonal","label":"Identify a fixed diagonal with the same midpoint","methodDirectNodeIds":{"fixed-parallelogram-diagonal":["cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_055"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_055","cnm_064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 4/5","reason":"Extend AB and CD to meet at X beyond A and D. In triangle BDX, the exterior angle BDC gives angle BXD = 46° - 23° = 23°, so XD = BD = 1. Since triangles XAD and XBC are similar, XD/XC = AD/BC = 5/9. Hence CD = XC - XD = 9/5 - 1 = 4/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"parallel-line-angle-bisector","label":"Construct an internal angle bisector","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-line-angle-bisector":["cnm_184","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_p210","cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_067"],"id":"construct-angle-bisector","label":"Create an angle bisector inside the trapezoid","methodDirectNodeIds":{"construct-angle-bisector":["cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_069","cnm_268","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_069","cnm_268","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_055","cnm_p210","cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_067"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 5","reason":"FG is parallel and equal to AB. Triangles EFG and HFG have common base FG, and their heights sum to the distance between AB and CD. Their combined area is half of 10, or 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_183","cnm_p148","cnm_p147"],"id":"shared-base-height-sum","label":"Split along a shared base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shared-base-height-sum":["cnm_p150","cnm_184"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_p148","cnm_p147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 150°","reason":"Normalize the side length to 1. Then A=(0,0), B=(1,0), C=(1,1), and E=(0,1), so CE=1 and CDE is equilateral. Convexity places D outside square ABCE, giving ∠E = 90°+60° = 150°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_10B_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_180","cnm_064","cnm_068"],"id":"square_and_equilateral_triangle","label":"Draw a diagonal to reveal familiar shapes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square_and_equilateral_triangle":["cnm_058","cnm_184","cnm_191","cnm_139"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_184","cnm_191","cnm_139","cnm_180","cnm_064","cnm_068"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (4)","reason":"The area is 2(a-b)(a+b), so (a-b)(a+b) = 8. These positive factors must have the same parity and satisfy a-b < a+b. Only (2,4) works, giving a = 3, b = 1 and a+b = 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12B_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"rectangle-side-lengths","label":"Recognize a rectangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rectangle-side-lengths":["cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_h173","cnm_p089","cnm_152"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_h173","cnm_p089","cnm_152"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: a+b","reason":"Let X be on AB with DX parallel to BC. Parallelogram XBCD gives XB=CD=b. The parallel-line angle relations and angle D=2 angle B give angle ADX=angle AXD=angle B, so AX=AD=a. Thus AB=a+b.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_067"],"id":"auxiliary-parallelogram","label":"Split the trapezoid into a triangle and a parallelogram","methodDirectNodeIds":{"auxiliary-parallelogram":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_184","cnm_183"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_067"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: √3","reason":"ABEF and BCDE are parallelograms because each has a pair of equal parallel opposite sides. Their adjacent sides all have length 1, and each has an included angle of 60°. Each area is 1·sin60°=√3/2, so the total area is √3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_183","cnm_080"],"id":"split-parallelograms","label":"Split the figure into two parallelograms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"split-parallelograms":["cnm_p150","cnm_184","cnm_p147","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_184","cnm_p147","cnm_174","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_183","cnm_080"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 210","reason":"Drawing through C a line parallel to AD produces a triangle with sides 5,12,13. Since 5²+12²=13², its area is 30 and its height to the 13-unit side is 60/13. The trapezoid area is ((52+39)/2)(60/13)=210.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_10A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_067","cnm_112"],"id":"solution:auxiliary-right-triangle:b746884d00fe","label":"Create a 5-12-13 triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"auxiliary-right-triangle":["cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_p149"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_p149","cnm_055","cnm_067","cnm_112"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (2)","reason":"Take E=(0,0,0), D=(2,0,0), and A=(0,2,0). Since AC²=8, the conditions allow C=(2,1,√3). The plane-parallel and right-isosceles conditions give B=(2,2,0) or B=(0,1,√3). Both have perpendicular distance 2 from DE, so the area is ½·2·2=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_133","cnm_h113"],"id":"parallel-plane-square-construction","label":"Construct a square in the plane of ABC","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-plane-square-construction":["cnm_h117","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h117","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_p148","cnm_174","cnm_133","cnm_h113"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 5","reason":"The circle has 12 integer points: four axial points and eight points with coordinate magnitudes 3 and 4. Removing the two fixed vertices leaves 10 points, forming five antipodal pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1995_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_h173","cnm_250"],"id":"diagonal-circle","label":"Count possible diagonals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diagonal-circle":["math:lattice-counting","cnm_186","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["math:lattice-counting","cnm_186","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_082","cnm_h173","cnm_250"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3√5/4","reason":"The midpoints are E=(1/2,0,3/2), F=(1/2,0,0), G=(0,1,0), and H=(0,1,3/2). Opposite sides are parallel, and EF is perpendicular to FG. Their lengths are EF=3/2 and FG=√((1/2)²+1²)=√5/2, so the rectangle's area is 3√5/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_10A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h151","cnm_055","cnm_174","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"],"id":"solution:triangle-midsegments:1424da505c3b","label":"Use triangle midsegments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-midsegments":["cnm_185","cnm_h120","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_p089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_h120","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_p089","cnm_h151","cnm_055","cnm_174","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"cnm_184","status":"mapped","total":24}
